Package: rTFT
Title: Hazard Models for Thermal Failure Time Data
Version: 0.2.1
Authors@R:
    person(c("Eduardo", "G."), "Martins", , "eduardo.martins@unbc.ca", role = c("aut", "cre"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0003-3500-9331"))
Description: Fits discrete-time survival models to the times at which individual
    organisms fail under thermal exposure (e.g., loss of equilibrium, knockdown
    or death), using Bayesian inference in 'Stan' through 'brms'. The hazard is
    the product of a failure rate that depends on temperature, following
    Kingsolver and Umbanhowar (2018) <doi:10.1242/jeb.167858>, and a baseline
    that may depend on the time under exposure. Failure times may be known-time,
    interval-censored or both, and may come from static assays, ramping assays
    or both. The failure rate may depend on covariates, group-level effects and
    other stressors such as dissolved oxygen. Static and dynamic critical
    thermal maxima, thermal death time curves and thermal tolerance landscapes
    are derived from the fitted model with their posterior uncertainty. Models
    can be compared by cross-validation that leaves out whole individuals or
    trials, averaged, and used to predict survival along a record of temperature
    and of any co-stressors recorded with it.
